-- title:
--   Exactness of floors when the degree bound is attained
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field and $F$ a field equipped with a $k$-algebra structure. Places of $F$ over $k$ are the valuation subrings of $F$ that contain the image of $k$, are not all of $F$, and are principal ideal rings; for such a $v$ and $g \in F$, $\operatorname{ord}_v g$ denotes minus the logarithm of the associated adic valuation of $g$, and a divisor is a finitely supported function from places to $\mathbb{Z}$, its degree being $\sum_v D(v)\,\deg v$ over its support. Assume: $x \in F$ is transcendental over $k$ and $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $k(x)$ generated by $x$; $f \in F$ is nonzero and satisfies $\min(0, \operatorname{ord}_v x) \le \operatorname{ord}_v f$ for every place $v$; $m, d$ are natural numbers with $d > 0$; and $E$ is a divisor with $E(v)$ equal to the integer quotient (rounding towards $-\infty$, i.e. the floor) of $m\,(\operatorname{ord}_v f - \min(0, \operatorname{ord}_v x))$ by $d$ for every $v$, whose degree attains the bound $d \cdot \deg E = m \cdot [F : k(x)]$. Then every floor is exact: $d \cdot E(v) = m\,(\operatorname{ord}_v f - \min(0, \operatorname{ord}_v x))$ for every place $v$.
--
--   This is the rigidity half of the degree computation for the floor divisor of $(m/d)\,\bigl(\operatorname{div} f + \min(0,\operatorname{div} x)\bigr)$ on a curve over an algebraically closed field: once the degree bound is attained, no rounding loss occurs at any place. It is used in the analysis of orders of functions on the modular curve, feeding [`ModularCurve.exists_ord_eq_zero_of_ord_pos_x1FunctionFieldC`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_ord_eq_zero_of_ord_pos_x1FunctionFieldC) and the two divisibility-by-$12$ statements for orders in the function field of $X_1$.
